…benevolence, then all are stirred to benevolence; when those above cherish deference, all are stirred to deference. Thus the transforming influence that advances the Way is duly accomplished. The ruler’s virtue certainly does not depend upon the assistance of the common people. Yet once the people’s virtue flourishes in practice below, the ruler’s virtue shines ever more brightly above. Is this not assisting him without deliberately offering assistance? This is what is meant by “he distinguished and brought order to the hundred clans,” whereupon “the hundred clans became illustrious.” Nor do the common people, in modeling themselves upon his virtue, seek thereby to serve their ruler. Yet once the people’s virtue uniformly flourishes in practice, the ruler’s virtue consequently spreads ever farther. Is this not accomplishing something without deliberately undertaking it? This is what is meant by “he harmonized the myriad states,” whereupon “the common people were transformed.” Such are the blessings that the spirits bestow upon our ruler!

Consider, moreover, the poem “Heaven Protects” (Tian bao). Its first three stanzas speak of Heaven blessing the ruler; its last three speak of the spirits blessing him. As for Heaven’s blessings upon the ruler, the wish is that they should resemble mountains, hills, ridges, and heights, and come pouring in like a river. As for the spirits’ blessings upon him, the wish is that they should resemble the sun and moon, the southern mountain, and the pine and cypress. All express the utmost loyalty and love of a subject toward his ruler. Is this not what is meant by “those above bestow in this spirit, and those below repay in this spirit”? Even a thousand years later, readers of this poem still feel its warmth, as though present at the Zhou court, witnessing the splendid harmony in which ruler and ministers, superiors and inferiors, graciously associated with one another.

Worthy men are the fence;
the multitude is the wall;
great states are the screen;
great lineages are the supporting posts.
Cherishing virtue brings peace;
the sons of the ruling lineage are the city wall.

Ma Li.
